3.2 Elastic Rope (Red Rope)
The red rope behaves like a rubber band or bungee cord.
Physics Primitive: Replace PinJoint with DampedSpring.
Hooke's Law: The force applied is $F = -k(x - L) - b v$.
   $k$ (Stiffness): High enough to retract the candy quickly, but low enough to allow stretching.
   $b$ (Damping): Crucial to prevent the candy from oscillating forever.
   Visuals: The sprite for this rope must tile or stretch dynamically based on the distance between segments.
   3.3 Visualizing Tension (Splines)
   Physics bodies are jagged. Use Catmull-Rom Splines or Bézier Curves to draw a smooth line through the centers of the rope segments.
   Tension Feedback: If the distance between segments exceeds the rest length (constraint violation), tint the rope red to indicate stress.
3. Anchor Variations & Mechanics
   The "Anchor" is the point where the rope connects to the world. Cut the Rope features several distinct anchor types with unique logic.
   4.1 Static Anchor (The Standard Hook)
   Logic: A simple coordinate point in the world space. The first segment of the rope is pinned to this static_body.
   4.2 Automatic Anchor (Blue Dotted Circle)
   This anchor creates a rope dynamically when the candy enters its radius.
   State: Active or Inactive.
   Trigger: A circular sensor zone (Radius $r$).
   Logic:
   Check distance $d$ between Anchor Center and Candy Center.
   If $d < r$ AND Anchor is Inactive:
   Create Rope: Instantiate a new chain of segments from Anchor to Candy.
   Attach: Create a PinJoint linking the last segment to the Candy.
   State: Set to Active.
   Mouse Interaction: Clicking the anchor destroys the rope and resets State to Inactive.
   4.3 Slider Anchor (Moveable Hook)
   A hook that slides along a fixed track (linear or curved).
   Physics Implementation: Use a GrooveJoint or a Kinematic Body.
   Kinematic Body: A body with infinite mass that is moved manually by setting its velocity, not by forces.
   Interaction:
   If user drags the handle: Update velocity of the anchor body towards the mouse cursor, clamped to the track's start/end points.
   If user releases: Set velocity to 0. Friction is effectively infinite.
   4.4 Winch Anchor (Mechanical Wheel)
   Introduced in the Gift Box, this anchor can extend or retract the rope length.
   Physics Implementation: This cannot be a standard PinJoint chain because the number of segments needs to change, or the constraint distance needs to change.
   Method A (The "Slide" Approach):
   Use a SlideJoint between the Anchor and the first rope segment.
   To Retract: Decrease SlideJoint.max distance. If max reaches 0, remove the top segment and re-attach to the next one (topology change).
   To Extend: Spawn a new segment at the anchor location and link it to the chain.
   Method B (The "Winch" Approach - Simpler):
   Treat the rope as a single SlideJoint (invisible) for physics, and draw the rope visually using a simple line or spline. This sacrifices the "wrapping around corners" ability but is easier to code.
   Recommendation: For high fidelity, use Method A. It preserves the ability of the rope to drape over obstacles while retracting.
4. The Cutting Mechanic: Computational Geometry
   The core interaction—cutting—is a geometric query against the physics state.
   5.1 Segment-Segment Intersection
   When the user swipes the screen, they define a "Cut Line" from $(x_{prev}, y_{prev})$ to $(x_{curr}, y_{curr})$.
   Raycast: Perform space.segment_query(start, end,...) in Pymunk.
   Filter: Only check against shapes tagged as "Rope Segment".
   Topology Modification:
   Identify the PinJoint connecting the hit segment to its neighbor.
   Remove the Joint from the space.
   Fade Out: The severed part of the rope should fade opacity and be removed after 1-2 seconds.4
5. Advanced Mechanics: Gadgets & Toys
   6.1 Bubbles: Anti-Gravity
   Bubbles float the candy upwards.
   Physics: Apply an upward force $\mathbf{F}_{lift} > \mathbf{F}_{gravity}$.
   Damping: Apply linear drag $\mathbf{F}_{drag} = -c \mathbf{v}$ to reach a terminal velocity (bubbles don't accelerate forever).
   Interaction: Tapping the bubble destroys the bubble body and removes the PivotJoint holding the candy.
   6.2 Air Cushions (Fart Guns)
   Apply a directional impulse.
   Logic:
   On Click: Play animation.
   Calculate vector $\mathbf{d}$ from Cushion to Candy.
   If distance $|\mathbf{d}| < \text{Range}$ and angle is within Cone:
   Apply Impulse $\mathbf{J} = \frac{Force}{|\mathbf{d}|} \times \hat{\mathbf{d}}$.
   Note: The force decreases with distance (falloff).
   6.3 Magic Hats (Teleportation)
   Teleport the candy while preserving momentum relative to the hat's orientation.
   Math:
   Detect collision with Hat A.
   Calculate relative velocity $\mathbf{v}_{in}$.
   Get rotation difference $\Delta \theta = \theta_B - \theta_A$.
   Rotate velocity vector: $\mathbf{v}_{out} = \text{Rotate}(\mathbf{v}_{in}, \Delta \theta)$.
   Set Candy Position to Hat B Position + Offset (to avoid immediate re-trigger).
   Set Candy Velocity to $\mathbf{v}_{out}$.5
   6.4 Spiders
   Antagonists that crawl down the rope.
   AI Logic: Spiders are kinematic agents attached to the rope path.
   Pathfinding: They do not use A\*. They simply iterate through the list of rope bodies: Segment -> Segment[1] ->... -> Candy.
   Movement:
   Interpolate position between current segment $i$ and next segment $i+1$.
   Speed is constant.
   Loss Condition: If Spider distance to Candy < Threshold, trigger "Lose" state.5
6. Implementation Strategy: Python & Pymunk
   7.1 Architecture
   Model: pymunk.Space.
   View: Pygame drawing loop.
   Controller: Input handler for swipes (cutting) and taps (interacting).
   7.2 Code Structure for Rope Construction

Python

def create_rope(space, anchor_body, candy_body, length, is_elastic=False):
segments = # Calculate segment count based on length
num_segments = int(length / SEGMENT_LENGTH)

    prev_body = anchor_body
    for i in range(num_segments):
        # Create segment body (low mass)
        body = pymunk.Body(mass=0.1, moment=pymunk.moment_for_box(0.1, (5, 20)))
        body.position = prev_body.position - (0, SEGMENT_LENGTH)

        # Add visual shape (sensor=True so ropes don't collide with each other)
        shape = pymunk.Segment(body, (0, -10), (0, 10), 2)
        shape.sensor = True

        # Joint Selection
        if is_elastic:
            joint = pymunk.DampedSpring(prev_body, body, (0,0), (0,-10), rest_length=15, stiffness=50, damping=2)
        else:
            joint = pymunk.PinJoint(prev_body, body, (0,0), (0,-10))

        space.add(body, shape, joint)
        prev_body = body
        segments.append(body)

    # Link final segment to Candy
    link = pymunk.PinJoint(prev_body, candy_body, (0,-10), (0,0))
    space.add(link)
    return segments

